Recorded terminal streams, and the grid libkvt makes of them.

Each `.esc` is the raw bytes a real program wrote to an 80x24 pty with
TERM=xterm-256color: vim on this repository's README, htop, mc, less paged once,
and tmux with a horizontal split. `malformed.esc` is written by hand — an
unterminated CSI, a parameter past any bound, an OSC with no terminator, a
truncated UTF-8 lead byte, a surrogate, a DCS carrying rubbish — because no
program emits those and the parser has to survive them anyway.

THEY ARE NEVER RE-DERIVED. A re-recording picks up a different program version,
a different terminfo and a different hostname, so a fixture that regenerated
itself would be a test that changed its own question. The hostname and the clock
inside them are part of the recording, not something live.

EACH ENDS ON A LIVE FRAME, never on the program's exit. A stream that ended with
the alternate screen being restored renders to an empty grid — and so does a
parser that gave up on the first byte, which is why the self-test refuses an
empty grid outright.

`attrs.esc` is hand-written too: SGR 3, 9 and 53 with each of their offs, the
underline shapes of `4:n`, the underline colour of 58, and the four spellings of
a colour — an ANSI name, a bright name, an index above the sixteen and a 24-bit
triple in both the semicolon and the colon form. None of the recordings here
happens to contain any of them, and the colour lines are what pin the BOUNDARY:
the sixteen named colours reduce to the theme's slots and everything above them
is kept as the literal the program chose.

`links.esc` is the third hand-written one: `OSC 8` around a run, the empty form
that closes it, the same address twice — which must intern to one link — a
scheme the whitelist refuses, and a `params` field that must be ignored.

`prompts.esc` is the fourth: `OSC 133` marking two finished commands — one that
succeeded and one that failed — and a third prompt whose command is still
running, which is what pins the status landing on the PROMPT rather than on the
line the shell reported it from.

`vtrender.c` replays one into the 80x24 screen it produces, in small uneven
chunks so that escape sequences are split across reads the way a pty splits
them. `testing/goldens/vt-*.txt` are its output: the characters, then `--`, then one
letter per cell for the attributes — `i`, `s`, `o`, `u` (or the shape's digit for
`4:1`-`4:5`), `b`, `r`, first set wins — then `--` again and which cells carry a
colour of their own: `f`, `b`, `B`, `u`; then `--` once more and which cells are
a hyperlink, as the id itself — `1` for the first address a stream named, `+`
past nine. No colour VALUE is in a golden, because a value moves with the theme
and a palette change must not read as "vim drifted"; what is here is the
decision about which colours become literals, and that moves only when the rule
does. The link block holds the id rather than a flag because the two things
worth pinning are that one run is one link and that the same address twice is
the same link. Last comes one character per ROW for the prompt marks — `?` a
prompt whose command has not finished, `0` one that succeeded, `!` one that
failed — because a mark is a property of the line and not of a cell.

`record.py` is how they were made. It is here so that the knowledge is
recoverable, not because anything re-runs it.
